 "_interpretation_segmentation": "U-Nets (M3, M4, members, original-split ConvNeXt-S) neither overfit nor fit their training data well: knot IoU on their own human training frames is only 0.76-0.78, versus 0.75-0.76 on test, a gap of +0.012 to +0.027 (M4 ensemble +0.012). That is a high-bias, low-variance regime, consistent with the seed analysis (B1: ~95% of the error is bias). Capacity is not the limit (an overfit test reached knot IoU 0.975 on a small set); the limits are label inconsistency and the regularisation (augmentation, EMA, 12 epochs). The fit to the pseudo-labels the models trained on is consistently higher than the fit to human labels: +0.035 (M3), +0.045 (M4), +0.042 (original-split ConvNeXt-S). The students imitate their teacher better than they match humans, so the residual error is mostly teacher/human disagreement (outlines, thin knots), which more epochs cannot fix. MLflow curves agree: for every 12-epoch run the selected epoch is the last or within 0.004 of the best val knot IoU; no late decline (no overfitting), and train loss flattens. The mAP50-95 gap is negative (test higher than train-human) because all three human training logs are far richer in thin knots than log 10 (37-51% of GT knots on logs 1, 2, 4 vs 14% on log 10; per-log numbers in M4.train_human_per_log), and thin knots are hardest at strict IoU thresholds; it is not a log-2 effect (log 2 has the best mAP of the three). On thin knots specifically there is a real fit gap: thin recall 0.85-0.86 on the training logs vs 0.72 on test; instance metrics are not comparable across logs with different knot populations, so read the pixel IoU gap. M2 (DINOv3 784): lower everywhere (human 0.715, test 0.676, gap +0.039), so it underfits more (the 49x49 token grid limits outlines). M1 (DINOv3 v1, frozen, 320 px): gap +0.217 (human train 0.633 vs test 0.416); its 61 human training frames came from only two logs, and the val curve plateaus from epoch ~20 while train loss keeps falling (0.051 at the selected epoch 23 -> 0.029 at epoch 39), the only case of overfitting, on top of heavy underfitting. Caveat: on the teacher split val = test = log 10, so the selected epoch saw log 10 (selection effect, small here because the last epoch is selected anyway).",
